  "query_preview": "..Strong high pressure will be centered near the four \ncorners today, with slight weakening and an eastward shift for \nMonday. For today, precipitable water values are near normal for \nearly August with surface dewpoints in the 40s along/north of the \nMogollon Rim and 50s to the south. With daytime heating we will \nsee scattered thunderstorm development by early afternoon along \nthe higher terrain, with storm motion toward the west/northwest \ntoward the lower elevations of Gila/Yavapai later in the afternoon\nand evening. While the guidance doesn't indicate high coverage of\nprecipitation in these areas (PoP 30-50%), there is a good chance\nof gusty outflow winds impacting much of this region this \nevening. Isolated severe gusts (58+ mph) will be possible. With \nthe high still in place, an Excessive Heat Warning remains below \n4500 feet for the Grand Canyon and Marble/Glen Canyons until 8 PM \nthis evening. With slight weakening of the high, and additional \ncloud cover, we opted not to extend the warning into Monday.\n\nBy Monday, we are anticipating an increase in storm activity that \nshould last through at least Thursday. High pressure will shift \nslowly eastward as a weak trough approaches the west coast. This \nwill induce more of a south/southeast flow and allow for higher \nmoisture values to advect into our region. Forecast precipitable \nwater during this time is near 1.00\" along the Mogollon Rim/WHite \nMtns and up to 1.50\" for the lower elevations of Yavapai/Gila Co. \nWith this additional moisture, storms will be more efficient \nrainfall producers and the risk of at least localized flash \nflooding will be on the increase. In addition, storms may continue\nlater into the evening and even overnight, especially Tue/Wed \nnights. A disturbance is also shown moving up from the south by \nseveral of our models late Wednesday into Thursday which could \nincrease storm activity further. All in all, things are looking \npretty active Monday through Thursday, then some drier air is \nforecast to move in from the southwest by Friday or Saturday and \nremain through the weekend. This would likely not shut off \nconvection completely, but result in less daily coverage.",
